"}], "parent_id": "4646772f-f586-44c9-9280-b5c4636415cc"}

{"id": "e645a8e2-92ac-4f6e-815b-c93ce0e0c87d", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm noticing "Amazon Bedrock AgentCore" spiked to $13.06 on a single day, 11/16, as a new service — which could explain why it looks like costs tripled if the user is lumping it together with regular Bedrock spend. I'm tallying up the baseline weekly Bedrock costs from 11/09–11/15 to compare against this spike.

Comparing the baseline week against 11/16–11/22, the jump is closer to 32x than 3x, so "tripled" doesn't quite match — I'm checking other candidates like AWSDevOpsAgent usage, but those numbers don't line up either. I should dig into the AgentCore usage types, linked accounts, region breakdown, and USAGE_QUANTITY data for Bedrock, and consider whether hourly granularity or CloudWatch/CloudTrail logs might clarify what's driving this spike.

"}], "parent_id": "4646772f-f586-44c9-9280-b5c4636415cc"}

{"id": "6438519b-2db5-47a5-94d0-d854aac83f67", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "The AgentCore Runtime vCPU math looks strange—3.175 usage translating to $13.06 implies roughly $4.11/vCPU-hour, but the stated rate is $0.0895/vCPU-hr, so the usage quantity units might not be hours of vCPU time. I'll flag this discrepancy and move on to checking the CloudWatch metrics.

I want to pull metrics from AWS/Bedrock and AgentCore namespaces, check CloudTrail events around 11/16, and review the Bedrock logging config in parallel to see if billing really tripled somewhere in the data.

"}], "parent_id": "4646772f-f586-44c9-9280-b5c4636415cc"}

{"id": "9d5c2997-b3f0-4b34-8879-2e3deb201acd", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "Those events look like they're from my own agent, so I'll shift focus to write events for AgentCore instead, filtering for things like CreateAgentRuntime. Before that, I want to pull metrics: daily Invocations, InputTokenCount, and OutputTokenCount per ModelId across nova-lite and claude-sonnet-4 from 11/09 to 11/23.

I'm setting up the SUM queries across the eight models, with token metrics scoped to just the five that support them, using a daily 86400-second period since the window is within 15 days.

"}], "parent_id": "4646772f-f586-44c9-9280-b5c4636415cc"}

{"id": "01bda703-075f-4dfc-b9f0-fcc2f75b8706", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice Sonnet-5 shows millions of tokens used between 11/09 and 11/23, yet Cost Explorer shows zero cost for it under any service—so it must be billed somewhere else that I haven't identified yet.

I want to broaden my Cost Explorer query to catch anything with "Claude" or "Sonnet" in the usage type, without filtering for nonzero cost, to rule out CE lag or a promotional $0 rate. I also notice gpt-5.6-terra tokens appear on 11/09 and 11/17 but with zero cost listed too, which makes me suspect credits or promotional pricing rather than a missing bill—so I should check the RECORD_TYPE dimension for credits across all services.

"}], "parent_id": "4646772f-f586-44c9-9280-b5c4636415cc"}

{"id": "21aa4530-c272-45e3-be81-61a8775e876f", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I notice Sonnet 5 and the gpt-5.6-terra "mantle" usage are both showing $0 cost, likely promotional or preview pricing. So Bedrock costs didn't actually triple in Cost Explorer for this account — the spike I saw last week was probably driven by AgentCore usage instead.

Checking the weekly token volumes for Sonnet 5, both input and output tokens are actually trending down week-over-week rather than up, which reinforces that Bedrock itself isn't the culprit. I want to pull CloudTrail write events around 11/16 to see what AgentCore resources were created then, filtering out noisy Get/List calls to focus on actual creation events.

"}], "parent_id": "4646772f-f586-44c9-9280-b5c4636415cc"}

